1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,185
Chai Corner is busy now.

2
00:00:02,185 --> 00:00:04,435
Ravi has six people on his team.

3
00:00:04,735 --> 00:00:07,825
Every day he asks: what is
the best way to run them?

4
00:00:08,375 --> 00:00:09,646
Time every task?

5
00:00:09,646 --> 00:00:11,073
Write strict rules?

6
00:00:11,073 --> 00:00:12,778
Or listen to the team?

7
00:00:13,078 --> 00:00:16,138
People have argued about
this for a hundred years.

8
00:00:16,788 --> 00:00:20,432
Every big idea in management
grew from the one before it.

9
00:00:21,132 --> 00:00:24,114
So, what is the evolution
of management thought?

10
00:00:24,414 --> 00:00:28,322
It is how ideas about managing
people changed over time.

11
00:00:28,922 --> 00:00:31,660
Each new idea fixed a gap in the old one.

12
00:00:31,960 --> 00:00:34,611
Think of it as a road,
with three big stops.

13
00:00:35,211 --> 00:00:36,143
Stop one.

14
00:00:36,143 --> 00:00:39,462
The classical approach,
in the early 1900s.

15
00:00:39,762 --> 00:00:42,930
It saw a business as a
machine, with one best way.

16
00:00:43,280 --> 00:00:48,607
Frederick Taylor timed every task,
and wrote about scientific management

17
00:00:48,607 --> 00:00:49,550
in 1911.

18
00:00:49,900 --> 00:00:55,084
Henri Fayol wrote rules for
running the whole company, in 1916.

19
00:00:55,434 --> 00:00:59,568
Max Weber wanted clear rules
and a clear chain of command.

20
00:00:59,568 --> 00:01:00,556
Bureaucracy.

21
00:01:00,906 --> 00:01:05,118
At Chai Corner: one fixed recipe,
and one boss for every job.

22
00:01:05,468 --> 00:01:06,863
But there was a gap.

23
00:01:06,863 --> 00:01:09,077
It treated people like machine parts.

24
00:01:09,677 --> 00:01:10,678
Stop two.

25
00:01:10,678 --> 00:01:13,414
Neo-classical, or human relations.

26
00:01:13,714 --> 00:01:20,508
From 1924 to 1932, the Hawthorne
studies watched workers in a factory.

27
00:01:20,858 --> 00:01:23,605
They changed the lights,
the breaks, and the hours.

28
00:01:23,955 --> 00:01:27,849
Output often went up, because
workers felt noticed and valued.

29
00:01:28,199 --> 00:01:29,847
Elton Mayo explained why.

30
00:01:29,847 --> 00:01:32,375
People work better when they feel heard.

31
00:01:32,725 --> 00:01:36,130
So Ravi starts a five minute
team chat every morning.

32
00:01:36,480 --> 00:01:39,000
But one stall is part of a bigger world.

33
00:01:39,600 --> 00:01:40,594
Stop three.

34
00:01:40,594 --> 00:01:42,246
The modern approaches.

35
00:01:42,546 --> 00:01:47,883
In the 1950s and 1960s
came the systems approach.

36
00:01:48,233 --> 00:01:49,825
A business is a system.

37
00:01:49,825 --> 00:01:52,804
Inputs go in, work
happens, outputs come out.

38
00:01:53,154 --> 00:01:56,273
If the milk arrives late,
the whole stall slows down.

39
00:01:56,623 --> 00:02:02,855
Then, in the 1960s and 1970s,
came the contingency approach.

40
00:02:03,205 --> 00:02:04,991
There is no one best way.

41
00:02:04,991 --> 00:02:06,865
It depends on the situation.

42
00:02:07,215 --> 00:02:10,708
A calm Sunday and a festival
rush need different plans.

43
00:02:11,308 --> 00:02:15,042
From machines, to people, to
whole systems and situations.

44
00:02:15,692 --> 00:02:16,624
Quick check.

45
00:02:16,624 --> 00:02:18,950
Ravi says, it depends on the day.

46
00:02:18,950 --> 00:02:20,526
Which approach is this?

47
00:02:22,426 --> 00:02:23,377
Contingency!

48
00:02:23,377 --> 00:02:26,117
The right way depends on the situation.

49
00:02:26,717 --> 00:02:27,730
So, remember.

50
00:02:28,030 --> 00:02:30,793
Classical thinkers saw
business as a machine.

51
00:02:30,793 --> 00:02:32,628
Taylor, Fayol, and Weber.

52
00:02:32,978 --> 00:02:36,587
Human relations, from the Hawthorne
studies, put people first.

53
00:02:36,937 --> 00:02:40,724
And modern approaches see the
whole system, and say: it depends.

54
00:02:41,324 --> 00:02:46,103
Next time, each stop gets its own
episode, starting with Taylor's scientific

55
00:02:46,103 --> 00:02:47,003
management.
